# The ledger of `json/path`: what the JSON query libraries of the three
# languages let a program do, one block per capability, and what Rebel
# answers. None of the three standard libraries has one, so the yardstick is
# the author's of 2026-11-17: Python's `jsonpath-ng` 1.16.0 and `jmespath`
# 1.1.0, Go's `github.com/tidwall/gjson` v1.20.4, and Rust's `serde_json_path`
# 0.7.2, the one of the three written to RFC 9535. The lists are this
# machine's, 2026-11-17: `dir()` of the Python modules installed in the scratch
# folder, `go doc -all` of the Go module fetched there, and the item list
# docs.rs prints for the crate. The tests are the JSONPath Compliance Test
# Suite (github.com/jsonpath-standard/jsonpath-compliance-test-suite, commit
# 20ebdcce of 2026-10-14), every one of its 715 cases.

capability path/parse  a query read, checked against RFC 9535's grammar and its rules of types
  heroes  json/path.parse, json/path.is_valid, json/path.Query
  go      -
  rust    JsonPath, JsonPath::parse, ParseError
  python  jsonpath_ng.parse, jsonpath_ng.ext.parse, JSONPath, JSONPathError, jsonpath_ng.lexer, jsonpath_ng.parser, jsonpath_ng.exceptions
  note    RFC 9535 and nothing wider: the grammar of its Appendix A, its rules of types, I-JSON's range for an index, no blank before `$` or after the end. The Compliance Test Suite's 249 queries to refuse are refused. A query's own nesting is bounded by `MAX_NESTING`, 64, which neither Rust nor the RFC states

capability path/select  the values a query selects in a value, in order
  heroes  json/path.select
  go      -
  rust    JsonPath::query, NodeList, NodeList::all, NodeList::iter, NodeList::len, NodeList::is_empty, NodeList::get, NodeList::first, NodeList::last, Nodes, JsonPathExt, JsonPathExt::json_path
  python  JSONPath.find, DatumInContext, DatumInContext.value
  note    the RFC's order: an object's members in the order the object keeps, which is the order they were written, one of the orders the suite allows wherever it leaves the order open; the suite's 466 queries with a result all pass

capability path/locations  each value selected with where it stands, as a normalized path and a JSON pointer
  heroes  json/path.select_nodes, json/path.Node, json/path.to_pointer
  go      Result.Path, Result.Paths, Result.Index, Result.Indexes
  rust    JsonPath::query_located, LocatedNode, LocatedNodeList, Locations, NormalizedPath, NormalizedPath::to_json_pointer, PathElement
  python  DatumInContext.full_path, DatumInContext.path, DatumInContext.context, DatumInContext.in_context, DatumInContext.id_pseudopath, AutoIdForDatum, auto_id_field
  note    each node with its normalized path, RFC 9535 § 2.7, and its JSON pointer, as Rust's `to_json_pointer` gives one; `to_pointer` reads only the normalized form back, refusing a path in double quotes or with an escape the form does not write

capability path/one  the one value a query selects, failing where it selects none or several
  heroes  json/path.select_one
  go      -
  rust    NodeList::exactly_one, NodeList::at_most_one, ExactlyOneError, AtMostOneError
  python  -
  note    none is `not_found` and several `invalid`, Rust's two errors in the closed vocabulary of codes

capability path/functions  the function extensions of RFC 9535: length, count, match, search and value
  heroes  json/path.parse (the five functions of RFC 9535 § 2.4)
  go      -
  rust    functions, LogicalType, ValueType, NodesType
  python  jsonpath_ng.ext.string, jsonpath_ng.ext.iterable (`len`)
  note    `length` counts characters, as the RFC asks; `match` and `search` read an I-Regexp (RFC 9485), translated for `regex`, so they run in time linear in the text, and a pattern that is no I-Regexp makes them false, as the RFC asks; `^` and `$` anchor, as the suite expects, where RFC 9485's grammar reads them as characters

capability path/update  every place a query selects given a new value
  heroes  json/path.replace_all, json/path.replace_all_by
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  JSONPath.update, JSONPath.update_or_create, JSONPath.find_or_create, JSONPath.filter
  note    each place selected once, the deepest first, so where a place selected holds another the outer one's new value stands; jsonpath-ng's `update_or_create` and `find_or_create`, which make the places a query names, are not done: a filter or a slice names no place that does not exist

capability path/custom-functions  a function of the program's own called from a filter
  heroes  refused: a query is text other programs read too, and a function only this program registers makes it mean nothing to them; RFC 9535 leaves new functions to its IANA registry, whose five are here
  go      AddModifier, ModifierExists, DisableModifiers
  rust    function (the attribute macro)
  python  FilterFunction
  note    gjson's modifiers and Rust's attribute both extend the language one program at a time

capability path/beyond-rfc  the selectors of jsonpath-ng past RFC 9535: the parent, arithmetic, sorting, `this`
  heroes  refused: past RFC 9535, which is the language this package reads; jsonpath-ng's parent operator, arithmetic and sorting have no standard
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  Parent, This, Where, WhereNot, jsonpath_ng.ext.arithmetic, jsonpath_ng.ext.filter, jsonpath_ng.ext.iterable, Root, Child, Descendants, Fields, Index, Slice, Wildcard, Container, LIST_KEY, NOT_SET
  note    -

capability path/gjson-syntax  Go gjson's own path syntax, its `#` queries and its `@` modifiers
  heroes  refused: another query language, gjson's own, with no standard behind it; RFC 9535 is the IETF's
  go      Get, GetBytes, GetMany, GetManyBytes, Parse, ParseBytes, Result, Result.Get, Result.Exists, Result.Array, Result.Map, Result.ForEach, Result.All, Result.Keys, Result.Values, Result.Value, Result.String, Result.Int, Result.Uint, Result.Float, Result.Bool, Result.Time, Result.IsArray, Result.IsObject, Result.IsBool, Result.Less, Type, Type.String, Null, False, Number, String, True, JSON, Escape
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    gjson reads a text without reading it into a value; here a text is read once by `json.parse` and queried as a value

capability path/jmespath  JMESPath, a query language of projections, pipes and functions
  heroes  refused: another query language, of projections, pipes and functions, a package of its own and not a part of JSONPath's
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  jmespath.search, jmespath.compile, jmespath.Options, jmespath.functions, jmespath.ast, jmespath.visitor, jmespath.exceptions
  note    -

dropped
  go      Valid, ValidBytes: whether a text is JSON, which is `json.is_valid`
  go      ForEachLine: values one after another in a text, which is `json.parse_next`
  go      AppendJSONString, SetEscapeHTML, DisableEscapeHTML: a string written as JSON, which is `json.quote` and `json.FormatOptions`
  python  jsonpath_ng.jsonpath, jsonpath_ng.ext.parser, jmespath.lexer, jmespath.parser, jmespath.compat: the modules behind the functions above
  rust    serde_json_path's re-export of serde_json: the JSON value, which is `json.Value`
